// ============================================================================
// RESOLUTION · BUILTINS  (a named library boundary, not a blind spot)
//
// THE DISTINCTION THIS FILE EXISTS TO MAKE. A call to `super()` and a call through an
// untyped parameter both fail to produce a client callee, but they are not the same
// kind of failure:
//
//   super()          the callee is known EXACTLY. It is CPython's `super` type, written
//                    in C. There is no Python source anywhere to point at, in the client
//                    or in the stdlib IR — VERIFIED: the 3.10.4 stdlib IR (31 shards,
//                    2,906 classes) contains ZERO py_type rows named `super`, `object`
//                    or `type`, because those live in Objects/*.c. So the chain ENDS at
//                    a library boundary, and that is what boundary_lib means.
//   f(x) where f is a parameter
//                    the callee is genuinely unknown. ambiguous_unknown.
//
// Collapsing the first into the second would report 7 of linkage-sample's 49 sites as
// analysis blind spots when the analysis knows precisely what they are. Promoting it to
// known_edge would be a fabrication, because there is no target hash to name.
//
// THE CATALOG IS EXPLICIT AND SMALL, in the same spirit as Java's junit_entry_ann /
// http_entry_ann knob lists: a builtin is only treated as a boundary if it is NAMED
// here. An unlisted unresolved name stays ambiguous_unknown, so the catalog can only
// ever move a site from "unknown" to "known boundary" — never the other way, and never
// silently.
// ============================================================================

// ── py_builtin_callable(Name) — CPython C-level callables ────────────────────
// The subset that appears as a CALL callee in real code. Types (which construct) and
// functions are both here because both are callable and neither has Python source.
py_builtin_callable("super").
py_builtin_callable("object").
py_builtin_callable("type").
py_builtin_callable("isinstance").
py_builtin_callable("issubclass").
py_builtin_callable("getattr").
py_builtin_callable("setattr").
py_builtin_callable("delattr").
py_builtin_callable("hasattr").
py_builtin_callable("len").
py_builtin_callable("repr").
py_builtin_callable("str").
py_builtin_callable("bytes").
py_builtin_callable("bytearray").
py_builtin_callable("int").
py_builtin_callable("float").
py_builtin_callable("complex").
py_builtin_callable("bool").
py_builtin_callable("list").
py_builtin_callable("tuple").
py_builtin_callable("dict").
py_builtin_callable("set").
py_builtin_callable("frozenset").
py_builtin_callable("iter").
py_builtin_callable("next").
py_builtin_callable("enumerate").
py_builtin_callable("zip").
py_builtin_callable("map").
py_builtin_callable("filter").
py_builtin_callable("range").
py_builtin_callable("reversed").
py_builtin_callable("sorted").
py_builtin_callable("sum").
py_builtin_callable("min").
py_builtin_callable("max").
py_builtin_callable("abs").
py_builtin_callable("round").
py_builtin_callable("divmod").
py_builtin_callable("pow").
py_builtin_callable("print").
py_builtin_callable("input").
py_builtin_callable("open").
py_builtin_callable("hash").
py_builtin_callable("id").
py_builtin_callable("callable").
py_builtin_callable("format").
py_builtin_callable("vars").
py_builtin_callable("dir").
py_builtin_callable("globals").
py_builtin_callable("locals").
py_builtin_callable("staticmethod").
py_builtin_callable("classmethod").
py_builtin_callable("property").
py_builtin_callable("slice").
py_builtin_callable("memoryview").
py_builtin_callable("compile").
py_builtin_callable("eval").
py_builtin_callable("exec").
py_builtin_callable("chr").
py_builtin_callable("ord").
py_builtin_callable("hex").
py_builtin_callable("oct").
py_builtin_callable("bin").
py_builtin_callable("ascii").
py_builtin_callable("any").
py_builtin_callable("all").
py_builtin_callable("iter").
py_builtin_callable("StopIteration").
py_builtin_callable("Exception").
py_builtin_callable("BaseException").
py_builtin_callable("ValueError").
py_builtin_callable("TypeError").
py_builtin_callable("KeyError").
py_builtin_callable("IndexError").
py_builtin_callable("AttributeError").
py_builtin_callable("RuntimeError").
py_builtin_callable("NotImplementedError").
py_builtin_callable("OSError").
py_builtin_callable("IOError").

// ── py_builtin_callable, DERIVED — whatever the pinned interpreter says ──────
// THE CATALOG ABOVE IS NOW A FLOOR, NOT THE WHOLE ANSWER, and the reason is that a
// hand-listed set of a language's builtins drifts the moment the language adds one.
// The parser hit exactly this and fixed it on its own side (Parser@2c2396f): its
// CALLABLE_BUILTINS was ~60 names and omitted EVERY exception type, so `raise
// ValueError(...)` read as an unresolved name — 2,235 stdlib sites in the gap bucket.
// It now generates the list from the pinned interpreter and emits `bindingKind=BUILTIN`
// (PD-5), so the engine no longer has to guess, and keeping a second hand list here
// would just reproduce the bug one layer up.
//
// MEASURED on a 77-file stdlib corpus (asyncio, json, http, email, logging, typing,
// dataclasses): the parser labels 98 distinct names BUILTIN; the explicit catalog above
// carries 78 and MISSES 29, almost all of them exception types — ConnectionError,
// ImportError, KeyboardInterrupt, FileNotFoundError, TimeoutError, UnicodeDecodeError.
// That is 23 of 9,130 call sites (0.25%) which were reported as `unbound_name` — an
// analysis blind spot — when the callee is in fact known exactly and is C code. They
// become boundary_lib, which is what they are.
//
// The explicit facts are KEPT rather than deleted. They are the floor: an IR produced by
// a parser build that does not emit BUILTIN still gets `super`, `len` and the common
// exceptions rather than silently losing every builtin boundary at once. Widening can
// only ever move a site from "unknown" to "known boundary", never the other way, so the
// direction of a mistake here is bounded.
//
// NOT EVERY BUILTIN NAME IS CALLABLE. `True`, `False`, `None`, `Ellipsis` and
// `NotImplemented` are BUILTIN bindings and are not callables, so they are excluded by
// name — the relation means what it says. The exclusion is exhaustive: those five are
// the complete set of non-callable public builtins in the pinned interpreter.
py_builtin_noncallable("True").
py_builtin_noncallable("False").
py_builtin_noncallable("None").
py_builtin_noncallable("Ellipsis").
py_builtin_noncallable("NotImplemented").

py_builtin_callable(n) :-
    binding_decl(_, n, _, "BUILTIN", _, _),
    n != "",
    !py_builtin_noncallable(n).

// ── py_builtin_dynamic(Name) — the escape hatches ────────────────────────────
// These do not merely lack Python source; they make the PROGRAM unanalysable at that
// point. A call to getattr/eval/exec means the engine cannot know what runs, and the
// schema marks the sites (callKind=DYNAMIC_CALL) precisely so it can be said out loud.
// Reported separately from an ordinary builtin boundary.
py_builtin_dynamic("getattr").
py_builtin_dynamic("setattr").
py_builtin_dynamic("delattr").
py_builtin_dynamic("eval").
py_builtin_dynamic("exec").
py_builtin_dynamic("compile").
py_builtin_dynamic("__import__").
py_builtin_dynamic("globals").
py_builtin_dynamic("locals").
py_builtin_dynamic("vars").

// ── builtin_target(Name, TargetLabel) — how a boundary edge is written ────────
// The label is NOT a hash and is not shaped like one, deliberately: it must be
// impossible to mistake for a py_method FK, because no such entity exists in any IR.
// It names the CPython callable so a chain ending here says why it ended.
builtin_target(n, cat("builtin:", n)) :- py_builtin_callable(n).

// ── builtin_object_init_target(TargetLabel) ──────────────────────────────────
// The implicit constructor: `Sibling()` where nothing in A(Sibling) defines __init__ or
// __new__. The target is object.__init__, a C slot.
builtin_object_init_target("builtin:object.__init__").

// ============================================================================
// STDLIB CALLABLES WHOSE SEMANTICS ARE PART OF THE LANGUAGE CONTRACT
//
// A tiny, EXPLICIT model — the same discipline as the builtin catalog above and as
// Java's per-ecosystem knob lists. Nothing is inferred; a function is modelled only if
// it is named here, so the model can only ever move a site from unknown to known, never
// silently change an answer.
//
// functools.partial(f, ...) returns a callable that calls f. That is not a heuristic:
// it is the documented contract, and CPython exposes it as `.func`, which is how the
// oracle itself names the target. Without it, `add_ten = functools.partial(add, 10)`
// followed by `add_ten(5)` is a dead end even though the callee is written two lines
// above.
// ============================================================================

// ── partial_factory_path(DottedPath) ─────────────────────────────────────────
partial_factory_path("functools.partial").
partial_factory_path("partial").

// ── call_is_partial(SiteKey) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The site is a functools.partial application, recognised by the written dotted path
// against the catalog — never by simple name alone unless the name IS the whole path.
call_is_partial(site) :- call_dotted(site, d), partial_factory_path(d).
